        "Q3. Explanation of TermsIn the context of text classification (e.g., deciding if an email is \"Spam\" or \"Ham\"), here is what each term **represents**:$P(c)$ (Prior Probability):This is the probability of a specific class $c$ occurring before we even look at the document's content. It represents our general expectation. For example, if $20\\%$ of all emails in a dataset are spam, the prior probability for the class \"Spam\" is $0.20$.$P(d|c)$ (Likelihood):This is the probability of seeing this specific document $d$ (specifically, the words within it) given that we already know it belongs to class $c$. It answers the question: \"If I am looking at a Spam email, how likely is it that it contains this specific set of words?\"$P(c|d)$ (Posterior Probability):This is the final goal of the calculation. It represents the probability that the document belongs to class $c$ given that we have observed the specific text $d$. It answers the question: \"Now that I've read this email, what is the chance it is Spam?\"2. Why can the denominator $P(d)$ be ignored?The denominator $P(d)$ represents the probability of the document $d$ occurring naturally in the entire dataset (across all classes).When we are classifying a specific document, we want to find the class $c$ that maximizes the posterior probability $P(c|d)$. We calculate the score for every possible class (e.g., Class A vs. Class B) to see which is higher.Since we are looking at the same document $d$ for every comparison:The value of $P(d)$ stays exactly the same for Class A, Class B, Class C, etc.Dividing every score by the same constant number does not change which score is the highest.Therefore, we can drop $P(d)$ to save computation time and simply compare the numerators:$$\\text{argmax}_{c} \\frac{P(d|c)P(c)}{P(d)} \\approx \\text{argmax}_{c} P(d|c)P(c)$$"
